  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+49y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(7t+{\u03d5}_{1})$\n- $y=R{sin}^{2}\u2061(7t+{\u03d5}_{2})$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{sec}\u20617t+B\\operatorname{sin}\u20617t$\n\nNote that $R,{\u03d5}_{1},{\u03d5}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants.",
  "worked_solution": "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\omega t+{\u03d5}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\omega t+{\u03d5}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=49\\,\u27f9\\,\\omega =7.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(7t+{\u03d5}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(7t+{\u03d5}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}\u20617t+B\\operatorname{sin}\u20617t$\n\nTherefore, only function I could be used to represent  $y(t).$",
